<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jamshedpur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jamshedpur is an industrial city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, founded as a company town by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tata-steel">Tata Iron and Steel Company</a>. Named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamsetji-tata">Jamsetji Tata</a>, who planned India's first large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> works, it grew around the plant at Sakchi, which began production in 1912 using iron ore from Mayurbhanj. It remains a major centre of the steel and engineering industries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamshedpur.jpg" alt="Jamshedpur" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shahbaz26 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green_City_Jamshedpur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bombay’s cotton mills and the Tata steelworks at <b>Jamshedpur</b>, opened in 1912, showed the growth of Indian industry, but overall incomes grew slowly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…began a spinning and weaving company in Bombay whose finer yarn, spun from Egyptian cotton on American ring spindles, could compete with British imports, and in 1908 his son’s Tata Iron and Steel Company began a steelworks at <b>Jamshedpur</b>, its surplus steel promised a buyer in the Raj.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>Maharaja Sriram Chandra Bhanj Deo (r. 1882–1912) built schools and hospitals, opened a light railway and allowed Tata to mine the iron ore of Gorumahisani, which supplied the steel works founded at <b>Jamshedpur</b> in 1907.</blockquote><a href="/state/mayurbhanj-state">Mayurbhanj State</a></li></ul></section>
